<BaseLayout title="Teaching"><PageHero title="Teaching" intro="Courses that connect the foundations of chemical engineering with modern computational materials discovery."/><section class="section white"><div class="container"><article class="course grid-2"><div><p class="eyebrow">CBE 423 / MSE 423 · Spring 2026, Spring 2027</p><h2>Computational Materials Design and Discovery</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>From first-principles simulations to artificial intelligence, major advances in computational methods have ushered in a new age of materials science. This course surveys developments and limitations in computational materials design and discovery since the Materials Genome Initiative.</p><p>Topics include material informatics, high-throughput simulations, periodic density functional theory, material databases, machine-learned potentials, and generative artificial intelligence. Programming exercises and literature discussions reinforce the major concepts.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://registrar.princeton.edu/course-offerings/course-details?term=1264&courseid=018029">Course details ↗</a></div></article><article class="course grid-2"><div><p class="eyebrow">CBE 504 · Fall 2024, Fall 2025, Fall 2026</p><h2>Chemical Kinetics and Reaction Engineering</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>This course covers foundational concepts in reaction kinetics for the modern chemical engineer: rate laws for homogeneous and heterogeneous reactions, catalysis, transition state theory, complex reaction networks, and reactor archetypes.</p><p>The course merges historical foundations with recent advances from the academic literature so they can be applied to active research across disciplinary boundaries.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://registrar.princeton.edu/course-offerings/course-details?term=1262&courseid=001800">Course details ↗</a></div></article></div></section><section class="section dark"><div class="container grid-2"><div><p class="eyebrow">Beyond the classroom</p><h2>Lectures and explainers.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Explore recorded chemistry and materials science content through Andrew’s YouTube channel and the legacy Rosen Review.</p><p><a class="button ghost" href="https://www.youtube.com/@Andrew_S_Rosen">YouTube ↗</a> <a class="button ghost" href="https://rosenreview.cbe.princeton.edu/">Rosen Review ↗</a></p></div></div></section></BaseLayout><style>.course{padding:3rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}.course:first-child{padding-top:0}</style>
